details:
**What is missing.** The turn drivers now say why a turn ended. `cs-codex-turn` and `cs-claude-turn` exit 5 and print `failure class=<class> retry_after=<secs>` (SBX-044, SBX-051). That line goes to the driver's standard error. With `cs-<cli>-remote -b` it ends up in a log file on whichever machine started the turn. Nothing is left on the machine where the agent runs.

**Why that is the wrong place for a harness.** A harness that supervises many agents asks each agent's machine about that agent, in one round trip. That is how it already learns whether an agent is in a turn (`--state`, SBX-056). The reason a turn ended is a fact of the same kind, and today it cannot be asked for there. The machine that started the turn may be a different one for every agent. It may be another sandbox, and it may be gone. A harness that wants the reason has to find the right log on the right machine and parse a line meant for a person. One such harness does not read the reason at all. It treats a throttled agent as one that stopped, and it restarts the agent into the same rate limit.

**What is asked for.** Each driver appends one line to a turn log in the agent's home when a turn ends, for every exit code and not only 5. The line holds:

- when the turn ended, in epoch seconds;
- the driver's exit code;
- the failure class, or `-` when there is none;
- the provider's requested wait, or `-`;
- the first line of the provider's message, or of the driver's own error.

The log is append-only and bounded, so an old line is dropped and never rewritten. One format serves all three agent families, so a caller needs no knowledge of which CLI wrote it. A successful turn is recorded too. A caller can then tell that the newest turn ended well and that an older failure no longer applies.

**What it must not do.** It must not change any exit code or any line the drivers print today. `--state` must not write to it, because asking is not a turn. A driver that cannot write the log must still finish the turn and report it as before.

**How to see the gap.** Run a turn that the provider refuses, using `cs-sandbox lender fault <name> --status 429`. The caller is given exit 5 and the class line. Then look in the sandbox for any trace of how that turn ended. There is none outside the agent's own session record, which has a different format for each CLI.
